I wrote the wall strips incorrectly: `strip.position.set(x, 4.62, z); strip.rotation.y = rotY; strip.translateZ(0.42);` then weird position.z += 0. It's okay-ish: translateZ local moves along normal after rotation, giving two strips? But both strips at same position for i=-1 and +1, overlapping. I intended blue/red halves. Not critical but could look odd: both boxes of width width/2 at same place, z-fighting. Fix: split long wall strips by z halves. Simpler: one strip per wall run in team color depending on z sign? But addWallRun handles long walls with z=0 and ends. Could color based on x? Let's simplify: add single cyan strip for all walls, plus red strip for end wall near red? Hmm.

Better: in addWallRun, use strip material chosen by parameter. For long walls, split into two runs? Actually addWallRun draws whole long wall. Add two strips: one for each half along the wall (local x offset ±width/4), colored blue for +z half and red for -z half. But rotation changes mapping. For long walls rotY=PI/2: local X maps to world -Z? translateX(t) moves along local X = world? For rotY=PI/2, local X → world (cos? ) rotation y: x' = x cos + z sin = 0? Let's compute: rotation Y by θ: x' = x cosθ + z sinθ; z' = -x sinθ + z cosθ. For θ=PI/2: local (1,0,0) → world (0,0,-1). So local +X maps to -Z. Team blue at +Z → local -X. Complex. I could just make all strips lime/cyan and add separate red accents at red half via explicit boxes. Simpler: strip neutral cyan for all; goal colors already strong. Let's replace the two-strip loop with one cyan strip and add red/blue half strips for long walls explicitly after addWallRun? Eh.

We can modify addWallRun to take `accent` material and add a single strip. For long walls, call addWallRun twice (one per half, width 52) with accent based on half: blue for z>0, red for z<0. That also gives vertical mullions per half. Better. End walls: blue for +z, red for -z. Then remove the odd strip loop.

Modify:
```js
const addWallRun = (x,z,width,rotY,accent) => {
 ...
 const strip = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(width, 0.16, 0.2), accent);
 strip.position.set(x, 4.62, z);
